Z||000000||William||04-21-2000||11:45 PM||||Well the long list finally got down to dew strips and thats the plan tommorrow. The dealer has the outers in stock but the inners need to be ordered. I wanted to do them all but I also wanted to do it all this weekend, bummer. Do they all need to be replaced or just the outers? I don't want to redo this job soon. I'm also confused why every uses these special tools when the window stop can be removed and the glass just dropped down? finally I was going to ask about removing the crank handle but I see in the other post that there is a special tool to use. Any body got a way to do it without that tool and is it a one size fits all device? thanks guys!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fret Naught||04-22-2000||01:41 AM||||I used a button hook for years, a trick my dad tought me. but it just so happens I had my door handels off a couple of days ago and while I had them off I made a tool, took about five min. get a piece of sheet metal about 18 gauge I would guess, cut a rectangular piece 12" long and 1-1.25" wide. In one end cut a slot 1/2" wide and about 1" long knock off the sharp edges with a file and bingo you have your own special tool. Actually, the slot should be just a skosh over 1/2" wide.<BR>Have fun||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||04-22-2000||05:40 AM||||Don't know about the stopped being removed to get the window all the way down, but went to do the same with a drill on my parts car cause I was going to use that drivers side glass in my GT, as soon as I hit the drill button the whole glass shattered before my eyes. I was lucky not to get any in them! You can make your own tool in about an hour Will. That how long it takes JB weld to dry! Get your smallest wrench and a phillips bit. Either grind down the bit so it's half the size it originally was or cut in half with a dremmel. Place the short bit in the middle of the wrench and mix up your JB weld and smear it in so the bit will become one with the wrench. Do it on cardboard or paper so after it dries you can pull it off. There you have it...dew strip removal tool!||||reg||1
Z||000003||William||04-22-2000||08:11 AM||||Thanks for those tips guys.<P>Any comments on if those inner strips should also be replaced? Do most of you guys do both inners and outer at the same time? I'm headed to the dealer in a couple of hours and will need to decide.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||04-22-2000||09:27 AM||||I didn't replace the inners when I did mine.  They were in good shape and not scratching the glass.  They are comparitively easy though as basically you just remove the inner door panel and don't have to deal with any 'unreachable' screws. <P>Replacing the first outer seal took forever since I didn't realize what I was getting into. Found a picture of the tool on Rodney's web site, had a neighbor weld me one up from a piece of flat steel and an phillips bit.  Did the other door in less than an hour, start to finish.  SO, could probably replace the inner ones in about 1/2 per door.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-22-2000||02:36 PM||||I've never seen where inners need replacement.||||reg||1
